From a023216a4251c62ba16eca1da50cf39a95a546ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TinkerBell-90 <131240361+TinkerBell-90@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 02:54:14 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Shaders: console threads of 2-D and 3-D groups come from the
 wave counter

A console-lane program in a 2-D or 3-D group of more than one host wave
rounded its host wave's first flat id down to the wave size. That is
exact only when every host wave holds an aligned run of flat ids, which
GPUs that fill such groups in 2 x 2 quads do not give (#216). These
programs now take their console threads from wave_base's LDS counter, as
console wave64 programs on host waves of 32 already do.

The counter gave every host wave a block of 32, a partial wave too, so
in a group that is not whole waves the partial wave could take a block
in the middle: with a console wave64 program in a 10 x 10 group on host
waves of 32, ids 68 to 95 were never produced. A full wave now adds the
wave size, and a partial wave takes the last block, which the console's
last wave holds.
